## Using `database.py` (raw driver)

`src/{{ pkg_name }}/database.py` exposes a `Database` class that opens and
manages a connection directly with the
{% if db_driver == "sqlite" %}sqlite3{% elif db_driver in ("mysql","mariadb") %}pymysql{% elif db_driver == "postgresql" %}psycopg2{% elif db_driver == "mssql" %}pyodbc{% elif db_driver == "oracle" %}python-oracledb{% endif %}
driver.

```python
from {{ pkg_name }}.database import Database

db = Database()

# Create
db.create_user(name="Alice", email="alice@example.com")

# Read
user  = db.get_user(1)
users = db.list_users()

# Update
db.update_user(1, name="Alice Smith")

# Delete
db.delete_user(1)

db.close()
```

Use it as a context manager to ensure the connection is always closed:

```python
with Database() as db:
    users = db.list_users()
```

