## Migrations

Migration files live in `database/migrations/` as paired SQL files:

| File | Purpose |
|------|---------|
| `0001_initial.up.sql` | Apply the migration (CREATE TABLE, etc.) |
| `0001_initial.down.sql` | Reverse it (DROP TABLE, etc.) |

Name new migrations with a zero-padded prefix so they run in order:
`0002_add_posts.up.sql` / `0002_add_posts.down.sql`.

### Running scripts

Make sure scripts are executable:

```bash
chmod +x scripts/*.sh
```

| Command | Effect |
|---------|--------|
| `./scripts/migrate.sh` | Run all `*.up.sql` files in order |
| `./scripts/rollback.sh` | Reverse the last migration (`*.down.sql`) |
| `./scripts/refresh.sh` | Roll back **all** then re-apply **all** (destructive!) |
| `./scripts/seed.sh` | Run all seeders |
| `./scripts/seed.sh 001_seed_users` | Run one specific seeder |

The scripts read `DATABASE_URL` (or individual `DB_*` vars) from your `.env`
file automatically.

> **Warning:** `refresh.sh` drops and recreates every table.  Never run it
> against production.

---

## Seeders

Seeder files live in `database/seeders/` and contain `INSERT` statements that
are safe to re-run (idempotent).

```
database/seeders/
└── 001_seed_users.sql   ← sample rows for the users table
```

Add new seeders as `NNN_description.sql`.  They run in numeric order unless
you pass a specific name:

```bash
./scripts/seed.sh                  # run all seeders
./scripts/seed.sh 001_seed_users   # run only this one
```

